WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.728 --> 00:00:17.033
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History!

2
00:00:18.054 --> 00:00:23.996
I'm your host, Faye Moss, here to interview another famous face from the past.

3
00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:30.559
When today's guest was a baby, an illness left her deaf and blind.

4
00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:38.126
With a special teacher, she overcame her disabilities and worked hard to help others overcome theirs.

5
00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:42.811
Please give a warm welcome to Helen Keller!

6
00:01:01.196 --> 00:01:01.997
Hello, Faye.

7
00:01:02.097 --> 00:01:03.378
It's lovely to be here.

8
00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.480
This is my friend and companion, Polly Thompson.

9
00:01:08.561 --> 00:01:11.903
She will use the manual alphabet to translate your words for me.

10
00:01:13.324 --> 00:01:15.766
I'm delighted that Polly could join us!

11
00:01:16.546 --> 00:01:23.471
Helen, you spent your career communicating in one way or another—writing and giving lectures and speeches.

12
00:01:24.031 --> 00:01:27.794
But as a child, you had few ways to communicate.

13
00:01:28.474 --> 00:01:30.456
Do you remember what that was like?

14
00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:36.519
In those early years, I seemed to be in a dense fog, but I wasn't totally cut off

15
00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:37.299
from the world.

16
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.422
I could recognize and identify people by the vibrations they made as they walked.

17
00:01:43.143 --> 00:01:46.365
I could also recognize people and objects by their smell.

18
00:01:47.525 --> 00:01:53.008
Even before I learned sign language, I had my own signs for when I was hungry or sleepy.

19
00:01:54.529 --> 00:01:58.852
Eventually your parents called on a very famous person for advice.

20
00:02:00.448 --> 00:02:02.910
Yes! My parents took me to visit Dr.

21
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.632
Alexander Graham Bell when I was six years old.

22
00:02:06.973 --> 00:02:12.218
He was well known not only for inventing the telephone but also for finding ways to help the

23
00:02:12.258 --> 00:02:13.178
hearing impaired.

24
00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.964
As I sat on his lap and felt his long beard, I could sense his kindness.

25
00:02:21.105 --> 00:02:25.428
He recommended that my parents hire a tutor from the Perkins School for the Blind.

26
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.765
And that tutor was Anne Sullivan, who was herself vision impaired.

27
00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:35.807
She came to your home to teach you.

28
00:02:36.587 --> 00:02:41.809
You call the day you met Anne in 1887 your soul's birthday.

29
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:43.530
That's right.

30
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.971
But our relationship didn't start off well.

31
00:02:48.052 --> 00:02:51.133
When Anne first came to us, I was a spoiled child.

32
00:02:52.854 --> 00:02:56.456
My family let me do whatever I liked because they felt sorry for me.

33
00:02:58.588 --> 00:03:01.829
I often threw tantrums and hit, pinched, and kicked people.

34
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.331
I was easily angered since I could not make people understand me.

35
00:03:08.131 --> 00:03:12.292
Anne used the manual alphabet to spell words into my hand with her fingers.

36
00:03:13.472 --> 00:03:19.154
The first time Anne spelled the word for "cup," I became upset because I didn't understand what she

37
00:03:19.214 --> 00:03:19.794
was doing.

38
00:03:20.674 --> 00:03:22.775
I smashed the cup out of frustration!

39
00:03:24.435 --> 00:03:29.149
Anne tried to bring some discipline to my life, even though my family couldn't bear it.

40
00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.791
But Anne had a plan for that, didn't she?

41
00:03:33.811 --> 00:03:37.272
Anne and I moved into a cottage on my parents' property.

42
00:03:38.093 --> 00:03:43.195
We lived there, just the two of us, so that she could teach me without my family interfering.

43
00:03:44.575 --> 00:03:49.437
At first I just imitated Anne's finger movements without knowing that they meant anything.

44
00:03:52.659 --> 00:03:55.080
Then one day something happened at the water pump.

45
00:03:56.136 --> 00:03:59.398
It seemed as if something long forgotten was coming back to me.

46
00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:11.205
I suddenly knew that the shapes she was finger spelling into my meant the wonderful, cool substance that

47
00:04:11.245 --> 00:04:13.106
was flowing over my other hand.

48
00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.969
At that moment the world opened up to me.

49
00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:20.410
I had acquired language.

50
00:04:22.071 --> 00:04:23.792
You learned quickly after that.

51
00:04:24.704 --> 00:04:28.387
I exhausted Anne by asking her to spell the names for things.

52
00:04:29.348 --> 00:04:31.629
I wanted to know the names for everything!

53
00:04:33.151 --> 00:04:37.254
And I learned to talk, but sadly my speech was hard to understand.

54
00:04:38.354 --> 00:04:44.399
Eventually I attended the Perkins School for the Blind, and after that I attended several other schools for

55
00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:45.460
the blind or deaf.

56
00:04:46.621 --> 00:04:50.924
When I was 20 years old, I was accepted to Radcliffe College.

57
00:04:52.788 --> 00:04:56.972
Many people wondered how a deaf-blind young woman would manage at college.

58
00:04:57.753 --> 00:04:58.594
But I did well.

59
00:05:01.376 --> 00:05:02.437
I would say so!

60
00:05:03.078 --> 00:05:11.525
In 1909 you graduated with honors—and became the first deaf-blind person to earn a Bachelor of Arts degree.

61
00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:18.412
You wrote your first book, The Story of My Life, while you were still a student there.

62
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.504
The studies weren't difficult for me since I loved to learn and read books in Braille.

63
00:05:25.785 --> 00:05:29.688
I particularly enjoyed studying languages like French and German.

64
00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:37.094
But since Radcliffe was not designed for hearing- and vision-impaired students, I depended on Anne to help me.

65
00:05:38.976 --> 00:05:43.399
I was always lucky to have dear companions to finger spell and help me speak.

66
00:05:44.761 --> 00:05:48.724
After Anne died in 1936, Polly stepped in for her.

67
00:05:50.324 --> 00:05:53.465
You were also famous—even as a child.

68
00:05:54.306 --> 00:05:55.866
Was this challenging for you?

69
00:05:56.627 --> 00:05:59.308
Being famous could sometimes be an obstacle.

70
00:06:00.028 --> 00:06:05.891
But it also allowed me to meet many powerful and influential people, such as the writer Mark Twain

71
00:06:05.951 --> 00:06:07.592
and the actor Charlie Chaplin.

72
00:06:08.972 --> 00:06:10.213
I met every U.S.

73
00:06:10.273 --> 00:06:13.274
president from Grover Cleveland to Lyndon Johnson.

74
00:06:14.454 --> 00:06:17.776
I used these connections to advocate for the less fortunate.

75
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.961
So I think I put my fame to good use.

76
00:06:22.301 --> 00:06:26.942
Not many people know about this side of you—you were quite the activist!

77
00:06:28.503 --> 00:06:30.524
I was passionate about many causes.

78
00:06:31.464 --> 00:06:37.386
I was one of the founders of the American Civil Liberties Union, an organization that works to protect

79
00:06:37.446 --> 00:06:39.966
the Constitutional rights of the American people.

80
00:06:40.907 --> 00:06:44.528
I also worked hard to get women the right to vote, which they won in 1920.

81
00:06:46.632 --> 00:06:52.074
I helped workers fight for the right to organize so they could get better working conditions and better

82
00:06:52.134 --> 00:06:55.196
pay. And I was a pacifist too.

83
00:06:56.116 --> 00:07:01.478
I also wrote about issues that were important to me and traveled around the world giving speeches.

84
00:07:02.619 --> 00:07:08.321
I required an interpreter for this, as people sometimes found it difficult to understand my speech.

85
00:07:09.401 --> 00:07:11.282
This was one of my only regrets.

86
00:07:12.062 --> 00:07:15.924
I could have helped many more people if I had been able to speak more clearly.

87
00:07:17.116 --> 00:07:20.499
How did you come to care so deeply about helping others?

88
00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:25.602
Early in my career, I worked for the American Foundation for the Blind.

89
00:07:26.603 --> 00:07:30.465
In the position I realized that poor people confront many obstacles.

90
00:07:31.666 --> 00:07:37.470
I wasn't poor, but as a deaf and blind person, I certainly encountered many obstacles too.

91
00:07:38.711 --> 00:07:41.293
So I wanted to help as many people as I could.

92
00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:45.276
We all have obstacles of one sort or another, Faye.

93
00:07:46.368 --> 00:07:47.609
That's true, Helen!

94
00:07:48.069 --> 00:07:51.451
And now I must thank you for sharing your story with us today.

95
00:07:52.051 --> 00:07:54.912
And thank you, Polly, for assisting with this interview!

96
00:07:55.993 --> 00:07:58.474
I'll let you both get back to history now.

97
00:07:59.335 --> 00:08:00.095
Thank you, Faye.

98
00:08:00.435 --> 00:08:01.336
It's been a pleasure.

99
00:08:04.957 --> 00:08:11.113
Helen Keller remained an influential figure even in her eighties.

100
00:08:11.253 --> 00:08:17.718
In the early 1960s, an award-winning film brought Helen's story to the big screen, and she received the

101
00:08:17.798 --> 00:08:19.760
Presidential Medal of Freedom.

102
00:08:20.741 --> 00:08:24.243
She wrote a dozen books and gave speeches all around the world.

103
00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:30.188
Keller died in 1968, at age 87, in her home.

104
00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:38.375
Her ashes were buried next to her lifelong friends, Anne Sullivan and Polly Thompson, who had died in

105
00:08:38.395 --> 00:08:49.125
1960. Helen Keller's story of overcoming obstacles and advocating for important causes still inspires people today.

106
00:08:49.145 --> 00:08:52.106
I hope you enjoyed today's interview.

107
00:08:52.126 --> 00:08:53.507
See you next time!
